WEBVTT

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:06.060
AMIR NETZ: Hello, I'm Amir
Netz, I'm a Technical Fellow

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.030
at Microsoft and the CTO
of Microsoft Fabric.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.490
If you don't know
Microsoft Fabric, well,

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.020
you've probably been
sleeping under a rock

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.550
for the last 2 1/2 years.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:18.930
We released Fabric just over
2 years ago and it was all

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:22.740
about unifying all the data
services we have at Microsoft,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.580
whether they're operational
databases or the SQL database

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.820
and causes to be the no-SQL
database, the data factory

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.800
that allows you to
do data integration,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.740
analytics covers everything
from data warehousing

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.130
to data engineering to data
science, real-time intelligence

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:42.090
with ability to bring live
event streams and manage them

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.570
through event houses and act
on them through the activator,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.100
Power BI, which is, of course,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.800
the founding father
of the entire stack.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.810
Copilot is a foundation
capability for the entire stack,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.600
governance to a catalogue
and, of course, OneLake,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.590
the one place we store all
your data for the enterprise.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:05.670
We have released it and we're
incredibly, incredibly thrilled

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.650
to share some of
the latest figures

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.170
of the adoptions
around the market.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.970
The market really draws Fabric,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.760
35,000 customers are
using Fabric today

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.860
and we don't the Bar BI guys.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.140
These are the people who are
just using all the other

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.760
services that we have in Fabric.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.940
Over 90% of the Fortune
500 are using Fabric

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.690
and you can learn all about
those customers in this URL.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.510
Also, the financial results
are pretty impressive,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:34.860
over $2 billion in ARR, with
60% year over year growth.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.650
This is just remarkable for
a platform that has been

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.690
around for just over 2 years.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.210
But the world is changing.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.350
We are now predicting

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.950
over a billion agents
joining the workforce

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.240
over the next 2 years and that's
really, really important,

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.580
because when we say,
joining the workforce,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.740
means that they're
really going to move

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.480
from being just chat bots or
applications on your desktop

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.670
to real full-fledged employees.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.220
And one of the questions we're
going to ask ourselves is,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.520
how do we make sure that the
agents can become this trusted

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.490
and this productive as
your human employees?

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.240
And the answer is, well, we
have to give them the tool,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.100
the knowledge, the
understanding,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.850
the context that we give to
all of our human employees.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:21.980
And when we look at, what
is this kind of context

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.260
and knowledge that we give
to the human employees,

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:27.890
we kind of categories
it into three buckets.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.590
We have the notion of the
people, every employee works

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.570
in a team, we need
every employee

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.040
to understand their teammates,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.840
to understand how the
organization works,

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.440
to understand their
manager, that's one element

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.390
that we expect every
employee to know.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.720
The other thing is that every
organization works differently,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.270
every organization has its own
standard operating procedure,

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.740
it's own institutional
knowledge,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.630
its own way of doing things,

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.930
and we need every employee
to know that as well.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.510
Lastly, we need every employee
to know what is going

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.060
on right now in the business,
what orders are we working on,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.190
what support cases
we need to resolve,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.860
what is the latest initiative
that we need to know

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.240
to save costs, all of that
is the most dynamic part

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.180
of the context of every
employee needs to know.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.670
And this set of context is
also what we expect our agents

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:18.300
to know.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.910
And this is why we introduce
at Microsoft something called,

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.910
Microsoft IQ, which is
made out of Work IQ,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.770
Foundry IQ and Fabric IQ.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:29.490
Where Work IQ is all about
how the employees work,

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.220
it's really understanding
each individual person,

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.520
understanding how they work,
understanding their preferences,

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.070
understanding their priorities,

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.080
understanding even
their personality.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.000
Then you have Foundry
IQ, which is really

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.460
about curating all the
institutional knowledge

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.140
that you have in
the organization.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.120
This is really about
creating knowledge basis,

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.800
that it tells you what it
means to be an employee

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:53.190
in the organization, what it
means to fulfill specific roles

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.140
in the organization
and how we work,

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.030
the standard
operating procedures,

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.510
the regulation we have to
abide by and so forth.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.680
And likely, lastly, Fabric IQ,
this is really about the state

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.010
of the business, this is really
about getting the real-time feed

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:11.490
about how the organization
is performing right now,

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.590
what is going on,
it's all coming

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.590
from the operational systems
of the organization.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:21.660
When we talk about Fabric, we're
really talking about bringing

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.300
and creating that
Fabric IQ and for this,

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.810
we have introduced a
whole new workload just

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:31.320
in the last few months,
which is called, Fabric IQ.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:34.020
And we're going to talk about
how you create Fabric IQ,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.450
it's not as trivial
as one can imagine,

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.730
it actually requires
some thought, some work,

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:42.240
some planning and the entire
stack is really about creating

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.340
that IQ, that context for the
AI that gives it a full picture

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.470
of everything that is going
on in the organization.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.780
I'm going to show you here
some of the innovations

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:52.740
that we're bringing to build

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.230
through the framework
of the Fabric IQ.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:56.430
And in the framework

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.730
of the Fabric IQ it has
-- we have four steps.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:03.060
First, it's all about
unifying the data state,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.230
bringing all the data together.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.810
Then we want to process
and harmonize the data,

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.170
so it all kind of matches
up and fits together.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.600
Then we're going to curate
semantic knowledge,

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.700
so the AI will understand
actually the data.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.700
And, lastly, we're going to
be able to put some AI agents

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.200
on top of those semantics.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.250
So let's go one by one.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:22.620
We start with unifying
the data state.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.470
And what does it mean to
unify the data state?

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:29.400
In Fabric we have this one place
where we can put all the data,

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.860
it's called, OneLake, it's
a single secure foundation

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.740
for every workload, for
every piece of data.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.990
It's one place where
all the data is stored,

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.870
all the data is stored in open
format, all the data is indexed,

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:42.720
all the data is managed,
we manage the lineage,

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.340
we manage the
sensitivity labels,

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.130
we manage the certification
of the data, it's one place

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.740
for all the data,
which is just amazing.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.590
But the other thing that is
really good about OneLake is,

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.900
it's not just a place to
store data, it's a place

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.150
to bring all the data
you already have stored

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.290
somewhere else.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.570
And we know organizations
have data everywhere.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.270
You have data across multiple
clouds, you have data in Azure

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:06.750
and AWS and GCP, you have
data in different databases

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:10.020
and data verses and in SQL
databases and data bricks

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.380
and Snowflake and in
[inaudible] and [inaudible],

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.570
you have data everywhere,
you have data on-premises,

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.670
you have data in
applications, and you want

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.680
to bring all the data together.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.740
And this is really important,
because data has this terrible,

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:27.240
terrible quality
of, left by itself,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.680
it will just silo itself.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.540
It will be stored in
different containers,

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.640
of different platforms,
different technologies

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.480
and then it just get shorted and
isolated and it's really hard

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:40.140
for humans to understand the
entire picture when the data is

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.770
so shorted and it's even harder
for the AI to understand it,

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.770
because for the AI when the data
is shorted it's like looking

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.260
at the world through a broken
mirror, it's just hard

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.730
to understand what's going on.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.970
So what OneLake allows you to
do is to create a single pane

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:59.040
of glass that fans all your
data, regardless of where it is.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:02.460
The data can be on-premises, in
every cloud, on all databases,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.490
in apps, in files, and you
could just bring it together

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:09.450
with Zero ETL, with zero
effort and with zero cost.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.240
So we have been constantly
working on adding more

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.880
and more sources of
data into OneLake

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.750
and just lately we announced
the general availability of SAP

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:21.720
in Oracle to be, and we're
really, really excited

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.790
to announce today
also that SharePoint

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:29.670
and OneDrive shortcuts are
generally available in OneLake.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.270
In addition, public preview for
Dremio, Azure DB for My SQL

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:36.780
and coming soon, Azure
Monitor and AWS Glue,

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:37.800
which really allows us now

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.610
to bring the whole observability
data estate into OneLake to join

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.340
with the business data estate.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:47.790
Okay, so now all the data
is unified in OneLake,

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.520
we're ready to go and
proceed into processing

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.650
and harmonizing the
data, really making sure

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.350
that all the data
can be working --

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.870
work together, the
data is consistent

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.850
and for this we have
this amazing set

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:04.260
of analytical engines, including
the data warehousing engine.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:08.820
And here we have an amazing,
amazing announcements.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.530
And this is the
query acceleration

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:13.590
in the Fabric Data
Warehouse through GPUs.

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:16.170
We are thrilled to announce
the limited preview

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.390
of this amazing feature,

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.970
which really provide you
much faster insight.

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:23.400
If you want to see the data,
it's just mind-blowing.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.440
The queries are coming
so much faster,

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.520
the setup is incredibly simple,
with one click, and really,

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:31.260
let's just jump into it,
let's just see what it means

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.000
to really have
query acceleration.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.450
So take a look here, the
TPC-H query performance.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.030
And we're going to compare
here a couple of competitors,

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:43.290
our own Fabric Data Warehouse
that is powered by CPUs,

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:44.760
as it is shipping today,

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.480
versus the GPU accelerated
data warehouse.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.450
And you can see that the
results are quite staggering.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:54.540
Typically we like to see
improvement in the 10,

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:58.140
15% range every year on the
Data Warehouse performance,

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:02.700
but we're looking here at an
8X performance improvement,

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.910
everything is running 8 times
faster on average and on some

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:08.040
of the competitors, even higher.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.540
So this is quite incredible.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:11.880
But that's not the end of
it, this is the performance

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:15.060
when you run single
query at a time.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:16.710
But what happens
if you now start

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:19.260
to load the Data Warehouse,
with concurrent load?

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:21.480
Well, the performance is
not getting any worse,

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:22.860
it's actually getting better.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:27.990
Now we're talking about
acceleration of 25 times faster

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:31.770
when you run 16 queries
at a time on top

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.930
of the data warehousing engine.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:37.560
In fact, it's barely
noticing additional load

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.360
when you work at this scale.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.000
Now, if you want to go to 30,

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.800
if you want to stress the
Data Warehouse even more,

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.190
let's move to 64 concurrent
queries running.

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:50.630
And now the acceleration is
over a 100 times faster.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:54.890
This is the power of the GPU
powered Fabric Data Warehouse.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:59.390
Not only it's faster, the more
you load the Data Warehouse,

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:01.640
the more gains you're
seeing on the performance,

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:04.220
it's just incredible,
it's a revolution,

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:11.030
it's based on technology that
just won the best paper award

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:14.550
in the Sigmund Conference,
it's just incredible.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.260
So let's take a look
at all that means

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:19.920
to actually have a GPU powered
Fabric Data Warehouse,

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.300
how's it look like in practice,
so let's role the video.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.150
SPEAKER 1: I'm a developer
building an application on top

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:28.980
of Fabric Data
Warehouse and I want

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:31.830
to make sure my users
get insights instantly,

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:35.100
whether they're viewing
dashboards or asking questions

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:37.080
of an analytics agent.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.690
Here we are in Fabric DW.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:42.960
I'm going to go ahead and run
a couple of ad hoc queries.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:44.910
At the bottom I can quickly tell

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:49.470
that there's a brand new option
called, query acceleration.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.050
It tells me that it's
off, but I can navigate

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:55.620
to workspace settings and
under Data Warehousing,

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:59.040
find the query acceleration
option and turn it on.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.090
And once it's on, it's
applied to every SQL endpoint

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:06.240
and every Data Warehouse
in this workspace.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:10.200
Using it is simple, I just
hit, "run," and I can see

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.320
that my query is
being accelerated.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.270
Monitoring gives me visibility

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:18.330
into what was accelerated
and what was not.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.120
From this brand new
column I can quickly tell

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.710
that several queries have
already been accelerated.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:29.400
Frequently run queries gives
me even more visibility,

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.640
with a count of the
total accelerated runs

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.400
for a particular query.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.100
And when I return to the
query editor and click on,

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:41.040
"query acceleration," I'm
given a birds-eye view

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.860
of the value this
capability is adding

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:45.840
for my particular
Data Warehouse.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:50.490
Roughly 89% of the queries were
accelerated, which is huge.

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:54.330
We're super excited to get this
GPU powered Data Warehouse

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:57.300
into your hands and
accelerate insights faster

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:59.760
than ever before.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.800
AMIR NETZ: So, using the
Data Warehouse is just part

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.530
of your Fabric application.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.150
And when you build your
Fabric application,

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.100
it will use many different
technology, you're going

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:10.290
to use the Data Warehouse,
you're going to use Spark,

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.480
you're going to use the
functions, you're going

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.370
to use the real-time event
stream, you're going

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.320
to use so many things.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.410
And to do that, we
really need to move away

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:22.680
from manually
creating and crafting

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:27.330
and curating each artifact
manually and instead,

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.490
move to agent based development,

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:32.340
just like we do vibe
coding for code.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:34.830
So, with this, we're
really, really thrilled

